#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e57f9dab-c5db-55d9-9b21-375bbc92281c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6814" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](images/47/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "9abded65-4a6e-5ebc-b496-d61b28179288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6818" :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](images/6c/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a6/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23009b11-9874-5e52-a6ca-94d3d3dae7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540588} {:answer/id #uuid "23f67f20-4371-571f-9fd6-447dacee9a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540594} {:answer/id #uuid "5e84fb78-cea0-5a36-8408-61ccd964b66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540688} {:answer/id #uuid "890c4312-8661-5c0b-8c41-8603b03d5f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540759} {:answer/id #uuid "c222c4b4-2ea3-582b-a422-05b9d5f10949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c222c4b4-2ea3-582b-a422-05b9d5f10949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540852} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f70034b-fd36-5c12-9141-c32f3cf668a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de318b1b-b80f-58da-a7ea-29f3d4789691" :question/math-academy-id "q-106396" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bccea09ccf18444f5b9505a8a753a9065ea97a5dc622208c281926c17cd5de1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,3),$ $a=3$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43baf2bf-555a-5a7d-80d6-1e80998846ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238642} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4bf5ea-95a4-51a4-8a22-9d7407ee588f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239118} {:answer/id #uuid "a87502b6-a974-55ef-9731-5c805fa819cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239520} {:answer/id #uuid "a900b4a3-b49d-54e8-8cd3-b7ee4fef1cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239531} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a791a8-6243-5cbb-8e82-29b536b3f82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87502b6-a974-55ef-9731-5c805fa819cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239520} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefc758a-5b74-5f60-8c85-a8cfa0ef9113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "face32c2-838a-5a8c-a2dc-dfdf5610f42d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106399" :question/problem "![](images/49/494e360a4c22d96feb811de6ee507996a11be65ee346d837debcb927c0d28434.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,2)$ with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f7a51d-051b-5f61-a319-e4c035a51a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540537} {:answer/id #uuid "279c72e9-1a9f-5c00-887d-cffe33962772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540596} {:answer/id #uuid "5aeea71b-7af8-5a52-acd0-a5f37cb0cba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540680} {:answer/id #uuid "baf87542-5fd0-51dc-a1ee-ff620017e69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540841} {:answer/id #uuid "c6715b4c-89dd-5083-9a48-108fad9694a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07f7a51d-051b-5f61-a319-e4c035a51a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540537} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75e67ad-7886-5cac-bd54-994109d0b711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6c896d6-3993-5114-bd3a-88e176c55230" :question/math-academy-id "q-106401" :question/problem "![](images/41/410d6ec438e066487cd6de9d4bcbf429362a5a86d5893e7820a832d2fffb9bbd.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/06/06e4a1a4d50943deff0b50eb510ca7ad4676ad6015a9503f7ce279f249078858.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c85e08-cf6a-5307-80f4-ce01ca0ebcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238422} {:answer/id #uuid "45c84b8d-f295-5003-a523-bb16c6bcf8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238658} {:answer/id #uuid "689800b2-34c6-5ad7-a7b0-9045f353fb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238964} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4de9d6-48b4-5098-a575-119212df9e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239570} {:answer/id #uuid "c35b39d0-2b50-56f0-b092-5a399f164c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35b39d0-2b50-56f0-b092-5a399f164c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778} :answer-field/id #uuid "45dde714-85df-57b8-888c-f62cc7cc39a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b47425b-d1be-54fd-a4d5-ddff6578df69" :question/math-academy-id "q-106407" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4db871-9264-5abb-8b35-f8cefa4d7edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540554} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca34a0e-8d0c-5de6-a6da-86fe375f2f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540603} {:answer/id #uuid "383804ba-6e24-59d1-8b86-33a991e9821e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540617} {:answer/id #uuid "389fd57e-2d25-5ed5-a585-2bc467281b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540619} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd2ee4e-8bd6-545a-9a0a-ae52bffe93ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ca34a0e-8d0c-5de6-a6da-86fe375f2f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540603} :answer-field/id #uuid "4318b039-4a2e-5f72-894b-9e7f5517fbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7435811-f6b1-5cc6-8105-467790c4f32b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15801" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1870c2f0-14d0-5419-98c4-385bdaed056b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1870c2f0-14d0-5419-98c4-385bdaed056b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a1a54eb-7389-5d8c-b81c-2180aaa70f10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55da411a-06c2-53fe-90b6-7e7539b042c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55da411a-06c2-53fe-90b6-7e7539b042c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540670} :answer-field/id #uuid "af419c4b-de5d-5ced-bcb9-7a2dd2a682bd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d2d95e-a1f1-5169-b942-8df45709b352" :question/math-academy-id "q-326264" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c5e30b2851a8268c5193c2f87e3c6ab77645db4d165d83150fb504ba06ef5c2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $H(6,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69716a57-1ad8-5016-aba3-02a3013b5d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69716a57-1ad8-5016-aba3-02a3013b5d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540708} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1988a8-3770-5656-84e1-09ce21c8ebb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c96a497-2573-5cae-bc10-aef22b9df685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c96a497-2573-5cae-bc10-aef22b9df685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540788} :answer-field/id #uuid "da64f4a6-5c11-512f-aa4b-74545a4bd468" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb1128f6-3c64-5af7-8173-a16416a853a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-326265" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbfc3c75c4dfeb96b6c17e8b3991238bfedde590297bbb551a0386b0a9508d00.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $10$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $I(10,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=10$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e167622-6728-5bab-98df-dcc27cb319d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos\\theta-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e167622-6728-5bab-98df-dcc27cb319d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos\\theta-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540792} :answer-field/id #uuid "917db9fa-14aa-58e3-8aee-dd6069fe2889" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1a663f3-d97a-5352-8f66-80fa9894fe7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin\\theta+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1a663f3-d97a-5352-8f66-80fa9894fe7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin\\theta+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540825} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fce734-0f4e-5bea-8f2f-5a31ade44110" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5bd0d286-b955-5de9-a7f0-4b2c20b02f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-327167" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a41c728f9a82b563203fa0eb6944617948cf12c048be2553b803b06820d0b896.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,4)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $6,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,4).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,4),$ $a=2$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fa5b866-e128-5753-a445-f1b463ce86be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^2}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^2}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fa5b866-e128-5753-a445-f1b463ce86be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^2}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^2}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540742} :answer-field/id #uuid "b240cd70-b283-509c-882a-a63367769e6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d95a82d-3596-5013-a69c-59119b644955" :question/math-academy-id "q-327800" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7e03f86-abab-5944-93ad-4c56076946d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{50}+\\frac{y^2}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7e03f86-abab-5944-93ad-4c56076946d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{50}+\\frac{y^2}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540920} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c37ef4d-2cd6-55e8-930f-488347a97b91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0a23b4a-b8a9-57f6-be01-ea66f5dec94e" :question/math-academy-id "q-327807" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{63} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300280} {:answer/id #uuid "611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{72}+\\frac{y^2}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{72}+\\frac{y^2}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540693} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdeb47f-480e-5ca4-a46f-7f342cc63ce7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4592b8-c042-55bc-b24f-4355e15f5f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327811" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18760c58-d102-5381-b95a-b10d5aa3c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540566} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce50a8b-77d6-59ce-836d-13b9fd87d4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540605} {:answer/id #uuid "2d10f3a1-f60b-5092-8a98-b5178f377426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540606} {:answer/id #uuid "ec610906-fbc8-5480-857c-9a008e48dd37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540925} {:answer/id #uuid "fc203c27-163d-5635-b0ab-260fa753b78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18760c58-d102-5381-b95a-b10d5aa3c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540566} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af6fa2a-61ff-5d6c-81a0-9fd99dddbae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9617ffa-95a5-5cf0-b858-c34d537f0472" :question/math-academy-id "q-6890" :question/problem "![](images/ba/baa8ce8fbfb4ed3acb3a9de52901b80e6acf81613e0c98ed04082fec3f653c0f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0268f3-fae3-58df-a8bd-8076695ac030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540572} {:answer/id #uuid "886ec113-7b60-5e77-8efe-36c5c735707f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540754} {:answer/id #uuid "ad707447-089b-5bd7-be1f-edc935e252bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540819} {:answer/id #uuid "d14209db-88dd-5983-9126-30629f8a80b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540880} {:answer/id #uuid "daa6d892-2a21-500d-8443-31bed71ef335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa6d892-2a21-500d-8443-31bed71ef335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540897} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b514b94-9f5f-5d34-a8ae-19775f141b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df871080-1b98-5262-9947-842390c2ab3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79274" :question/problem "![](images/00/008a79d8328b5b58cdc68a39a921125a1efa7e0b46a663b1ede9dc6773fb48e8.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/82/82436a7f248ed63683a3164d8c372433abb6f919567defa05700ebf88c50308f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a18201-8079-51dd-b682-32f608606117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345389} {:answer/id #uuid "2db8c32c-4882-57c9-b92f-dcdfe7b0c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757} {:answer/id #uuid "56f5788a-a5a6-5e32-946c-bf55db053ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346091} {:answer/id #uuid "8855c7fb-4098-5cd5-b05a-21eccba165f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346533} {:answer/id #uuid "b8010337-6cef-584f-b257-a06f6c10ce39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db8c32c-4882-57c9-b92f-dcdfe7b0c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea18173-d655-51a6-a7a1-312a50186ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de3651a5-52a0-5b3e-bfe6-600c2b9ae816" :question/math-academy-id "q-79275" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe1a9cf99e5a3b2cae80a4bfd01cdcabdc77560ccbb17ff9c7aa580a532d114.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5d/5d615d9fd29a09345b05ee4d80ffdc49198924d0cd44edb9c815270543fae60f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "748ac3a5-85cc-5953-936a-bedc1c90f253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540729} {:answer/id #uuid "7a45c712-d256-5991-8b85-1e21c37c1b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540733} {:answer/id #uuid "98499a59-a54e-55ee-813f-b3c78cd0eb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540782} {:answer/id #uuid "9d04eaff-79c1-5cde-9560-3f197faa247f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540790} {:answer/id #uuid "a834dcff-125a-5c3d-ad79-8fa365d3091d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a45c712-d256-5991-8b85-1e21c37c1b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540733} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e283730-f85b-5a90-97ac-ce8837023433" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bd7c7cd-dbab-5060-9d8d-5c720fc231fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79277" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc9e723494b5d592b97ff1b4795eb1bc0ca7be0c4608591792eb2ac5515a9811.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6eeff954ea6f159a3bdc781361da094319bcf06a5365d12e894228bb172a16e6.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200dc369-b95f-5003-9958-273f823a2f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345635} {:answer/id #uuid "6a200e6f-b5ac-52f6-972d-b2490377dfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346271} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5c6e0b-35eb-59a1-8aa0-be651e5193c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346461} {:answer/id #uuid "baa5e144-ff07-5209-b88f-ab79ce37dd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346972} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0019f-f524-5a56-94b3-fd7d7d44bbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0019f-f524-5a56-94b3-fd7d7d44bbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347573} :answer-field/id #uuid "90acc913-e06e-54ed-a8a3-00b923284f02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e37c1011-13e6-56e9-875d-43a2f977ac1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79280" :question/problem "![](images/75/7540559d86e836d869173da11ca0f7befba8ead07c844be1c9708591fb372042.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/30/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e4c87f3-4ce8-5f54-8e66-3cf5f2129b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540740} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e219d9-dd31-5e5b-8130-18f358b3c57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540865} {:answer/id #uuid "cd79da99-fcd3-5dca-bdef-4e1989a7fce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540872} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1911ec-baab-54e2-a03f-5da5527b6741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540875} {:answer/id #uuid "d9432e00-21b1-55f3-84fd-c22176423a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd79da99-fcd3-5dca-bdef-4e1989a7fce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540872} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6645ae1-82f3-5e16-a4df-5c345aba377b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db10a605-9f1c-5d1d-923c-41dc3d49c705" :question/math-academy-id "q-79283" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea74c15f3d02478a7486f967b7d2eebc780d1b5bd53ac21a81934b6438531f6d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15cf7084-0ec3-579a-9a37-96bb54f97e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345539} {:answer/id #uuid "1f910055-d120-5957-974a-884c85c30e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345627} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd7664-0cda-5513-96b9-d73b61d9c4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346032} {:answer/id #uuid "81d1d327-7a47-589f-87eb-a6542bdf2c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346486} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c7cb94-e4aa-558e-8677-1a6974d7d6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=64\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd7664-0cda-5513-96b9-d73b61d9c4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346032} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcacd8d-e197-5e83-9032-d75dbd8e30af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e66e328-95fb-545b-9b30-55b94d00170c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79284" :question/problem "![](images/da/dafaf384e39d336444f11ed4acea1f586058c990253e434ae148cbddebbe0abd.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $8$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(8,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2889b6-5645-5d8e-be53-45d82541f5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540548} {:answer/id #uuid "0f782b49-6ee2-54b8-a0c6-e6dbea2f2953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540555} {:answer/id #uuid "23c366f0-0d8c-5687-b051-7995725ed6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540592} {:answer/id #uuid "489612fd-3d5d-549b-bf4c-cf9add26f34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540657} {:answer/id #uuid "b150be25-e003-5b10-9396-dabb3aa842c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=49\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "489612fd-3d5d-549b-bf4c-cf9add26f34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540657} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1d937e-944d-5b73-91e1-cf60f6eae5c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11028c29-bebb-5855-afbb-0a0cead7b8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79288" :question/problem "![](images/13/13036c467cbe4fdd245b12c481de08ac1eb14bbfcc50653309a913ab1e9d8463.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,-2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} {:answer/id #uuid "32f648c8-3d4d-551c-a0fe-e98c1f6d2eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404012} {:answer/id #uuid "89052cbe-3422-56ea-9b9a-942801acf80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404013} {:answer/id #uuid "8b13ccd7-add3-5ba4-bf95-5440c51f18c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404014} {:answer/id #uuid "1c440c18-9297-5a46-a77e-a81bd6a4fec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9041eb-bfd0-524e-8602-d57198b3ea25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a6a1e67-ac65-5715-8684-1ea8d5d95e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-79292" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d45616d089e8f5d078ff48c949b26d0920074ab4b611ec71b491a0e18fd293c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,1)$ with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}]}
